{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>On the head or on the beard.</b> Scripture comes to distinguish between a lesion in a place of hair and a lesion in a place of flesh — the sign of the latter is white hair (שֵׂעָר לָבָן), while the sign of the former is yellow hair (שֵׂעָר צָהֹב), when the black hair that was in it turns yellow. <b>It is a netek.</b> This is the name of a lesion in a place of hair — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 13:29). And it would appear from this language that the distinction between a lesion of the head or beard and a lesion of the skin of the flesh is only what he mentioned regarding the type of white hair and yellow hair, for a lesion in its four appearances, when it is within the hair of the head or the beard — if the hair in it turns yellow, it is impure, just as it is impure in the skin of the flesh if it is white. But how is it possible to say such a thing? For he himself wrote (Rashi on Leviticus 13:43) on \"like the appearance of tzara'at of the skin of the flesh\" — that it defiles in four appearances, and not like the appearance of netekim of a place of hair, which do not defile in four appearances: se'et and its toladah, and baheret and its toladah. And if in four appearances it does not defile, then in which appearance and under what condition does it defile? And Scripture mentioned nothing in it except the signs of determination at the end: yellow hair and spreading.\n\nBut the Rav (Maimonides) thought that for the lesion of the head and the beard, Scripture informed us of its name, saying (Leviticus 13:30) \"It is a netek\" — and this is the well-known name for it, and by this name it is recognized. Now, Scripture did mention the names and appearances of lesions: \"white baheret\" (Leviticus 13:4), \"white se'et\" (Leviticus 13:10), and for the head it mentioned its name, and by its name it is known — but that name Scripture declared impure, and then went back and explained the signs of determination at the end. This is the Rav's reasoning concerning the netek.\n\nAnd so it would further appear from his words that the reason for the second section (Leviticus 13:40), \"And a man if his head be marot,\" is to say that when the head becomes marot — that is, the hair falls out and it becomes bald — it is pure from the impurity of netekim of the head, and that lesion called netek does not defile, and it will be judged according to the laws of the skin of the flesh, se'et and its toladah — for since there is no hair there, it is removed from the law of head and beard.\n\nBut all of this is not correct. For the head or the beard, while there is hair in them, are not defiled by the appearances of lesions at all. Rather, when a place on the head becomes marot and the hair in it falls out from its root, so that the place becomes smooth and bare of all hair, and there grows in that uprooted place thin yellow hair — that is the impure lesion. And that is why it is called netek (uprooting), because the hair was uprooted (נִתַּק) from there — not that it is merely a name, as the Rav z\"l said, but rather as its name, so it is: it requires the uprooting of that hair like a grind of grain (כִּגְרִיס), and after the uprooting of the first hair that grew from that place — if there then grows the stricken thin yellow hair, it is a sign of impurity, but not when it grows initially before the uprooting. And thus we learned (Negaim 7:1): If it grew on the head or on the beard, and the head and the beard went back and became bald — they are pure. The head and the beard before they grew hair, and they grew hair and became bald — Rabbi Eliezer ben Ya'akov declares impure, for their beginning and their end are impure, but the Sages declare pure. And regarding yellow hair that precedes, too, we learned (ibid. 10:4): Yellow hair that preceded the netek is pure, but Rabbi Yehudah declares impure. Rabbi Eliezer ben Ya'akov says: it neither defiles nor saves (neither impure nor a sign of purity). And this entire matter is also discussed in Sifra (chapter 8:6).\n\nAnd the reason Scripture did not mention the uprooting at the beginning is that it mentioned a lesion on the head or the beard with an appearance that is deep and yellow hair, and it is impossible for the appearance to be deep — like the appearance of a depressed sunken area relative to the surrounding skin — while the original black hair is still upon it. Therefore it described what is common among people, for when lesions come upon their heads, the hair first falls out, and a lesion develops in the netek and the thin short yellow hair sprouts in it. And afterward it stated \"It is a netek,\" to explicitly explain that it does not become impure until there has been uprooting.\n\nAnd the view of most commentators is that this lesion, which is the netek, does not require in it baheret or se'et, nor their toladot, nor any change in the skin of the head. Rather, once the hair is uprooted like a grind of grain (כִּגְרִיס) on the head or the beard and is torn out from its root completely — that is the lesion, and if thin yellow hair grows in it, it is impure. And this is the tzara'at of the head or the beard, for Scripture (Leviticus 13:30) declared it impure at the outset when its appearance is deep with yellow hair, and then mentioned again (Leviticus 13:31) that when it is not deep, it is to be confined, and declared it impure upon spreading (Leviticus 13:35). And the implication of this is that the appearance of the netek is not deep and not changed at all, and surely these two signs — which are the spreading and the yellow hair — are equal in their law. If so, the verses come to teach that whether its appearance is deep like the appearance of the baheret, or it is not deep and not changed at all, once it is uprooted it defiles through yellow hair or through spreading. And there are many baraitot in Sifra on this matter.\n\nBut it would appear that after a place on the head becomes marot, it is not a lesion until a baheret and its toladah or a se'et and its toladah develop in the smooth place, and then the baheret and the se'et would defile through yellow hair just as it defiles in the skin of the flesh when it turns white. For the verse that mentioned a lesion on the head or the beard and said \"and its appearance is deeper than the skin\" would allude to the appearances of lesions mentioned above, and it mentioned baheret and its toladah, and likewise se'et and its toladah. And this interpretation only serves to argue an argument contrary to our view — namely, that the appearances of lesions on the head would not defile except after the uprooting, and it has within it both leniency and stringency: a leniency — that they would not defile through white hair, and a stringency — that they would defile through yellow hair. And the verse that said \"And when the priest sees the lesion of the netek and its appearance is not deeper than the skin, and there is no black hair in it, the priest shall confine the lesion of the netek\" (Leviticus 13:31) — its meaning is like \"And if it is a white baheret on the skin of his flesh and its appearance is not deeper than the skin\" (Leviticus 13:4), for \"the lesion of the netek\" alludes that there were appearances of lesions in the uprooted area.\n\nAnd this requires examination in the Tosefta in tractate Negaim, for we learned there (1:4): \"And the netekim defile in every appearance, even white in the black and the black in the white, and they defile through thin yellow hair whose appearance is like the shape of gold\" — end of quote. It would appear from this that the netek requires the appearance of a lesion — either white on the black skin of the head like the appearances of lesions, or even a black lesion on white skin of the head like the black morphoea that physicians mention — for Scripture always mentions \"the lesion of the netek,\" requiring that there be a lesion in the netek, but it did not designate for them specific appearances as it did for the skin of the flesh.\n\nAnd the second section, \"And a man if his head be marot\" (Leviticus 13:40), teaches that the law of netekim applies only when the middle of the head becomes marot, leaving the hair surrounding the netek from every side. But if the back of the head or the front edge becomes marot and is uprooted from that entire side, it is not judged by the signs of the head and beard but by the signs of the skin of the flesh. And the reason is that it is in the nature of many people to be bald from a paucity of hair on the sides of the head, toward the back or the front, and the uprooting in them is not an illness but rather like the rest of the body. But when the hair is uprooted in the middle of the place of hair, it is nothing other than a lesion — and it is the lesion that in our vernacular is called tinia and in Arabic al-sa'afah. And Scripture excluded one who is marot from the front edge and from behind, for so is the way of people to become bald.\n\nAnd the reason Scripture said (Leviticus 13:42): \"And when there is in the karachat or in the gabbachat a reddish-white lesion\" — that it defiles like the rest of the body through the appearances of lesions, in thinness and in smoothness — and it said \"like the appearance of tzara'at of the skin of the flesh\" (Leviticus 13:43), that it becomes determined through michyah (quick raw flesh) or through spreading, which is the appearance of determined tzara'at in the skin of the flesh. And our Sages mentioned that it does not defile through white hair, as we learned (Negaim 4:3): \"The michyah defiles in the karachat or in the gabbachat, which is not so with white hair,\" and so it is derived in Torat Kohanim (Negaim, chapter 11:2).\n\nAnd there are some commentators who say that the reason for the sections is to distinguish between the netek and the maritah (plucking). And the matter is that karachat and gabbachat is when the hair is uprooted in a manner that it is not destined to return, such as one who anoints the hair of his head with a hair-removing potion, or it was uprooted naturally through the drying up of the moisture. And the meaning of the section according to this view is: At first Scripture spoke of the netek, which is when the hair falls out and is cut off — from the language of \"וְנָתוּק וְכָרוּת\" (Leviticus 22:24), \"the soles of the priests' feet were uprooted (נִתְּקוּ)\" (Joshua 4:18). And afterward it said: And when his head is marot — that the hair is uprooted through meritah (plucking), so that the head remains smooth and whitened, like \"נְחֹשֶׁת מְמֹרָט\" (burnished bronze), and in the sense that is written \"לְמַעַן הֱיֵה לָהּ בָּרָק מֹרָטָּה\" (Ezekiel 21:16) — since it is known that it will never return, this is not a netek, but rather this man is bald (kare'ach) or bald at the forehead (gibbe'ach) in the manner of bald men, and he is pure. But if there is in the karachat or in the gabbachat any of the appearances of lesions, it would be impure like the appearance of tzara'at of the skin of the flesh and with its signs — michyah and spreading — except that the Sages diminished from it in their midrash: white hair. And the reason for this is that the head has already been marot and is not fit to grow hair at all, and if a little hair happens to grow, it will never be black nor in the form of hair, but rather stricken and white. And this is the view of the sages of France in their commentary on tractate Negaim, and the matter of the mishnayot and the baraitot recorded there tends toward their words.\n\nIf so, the netek would be the illness of al-sa'afah, or the known illnesses called fox-machalat (מַחֲלַת שׁוּעָל) and snake-path (שְׁבִיל הַנָּחָשׁ), and all of them are illnesses and they have a remedy. But the meritah is the karachat, and it has no remedy or cure at all."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {0 "The Rav (הרב) throughout refers to Maimonides, here specifically to his views in Hilkhot Tum'at Tzara'at. The term כִּגְרִיס (like a grind [of grain]) is a technical term from Negaim denoting the size of a uprooted hair area; rendered literally. טיני\"א and אל סעפ\"ה are transliterated vernacular medical terms (Latin/Romance and Arabic respectively) left as Ramban gives them."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:24:49.494346498Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}